          "Chapter 3, Section 2 of skadeståndslagen (1972:207), the Tort Liability Act, provides that the state or a municipality is liable for personal injury, property damage, or pure financial loss caused by fault or negligence, fel eller försummelse, in the exercise of public authority, myndighetsutövning, for which the state or municipality is responsible, a provision narrower on its face than Spain's no-fault responsabilidad patrimonial but broader in practice than it might first appear, since Swedish courts have developed a comparatively demanding standard of what counts as acceptable conduct for an authority exercising power over an individual, closer in spirit to a strict professional standard than to an ordinary negligence inquiry. Liability specifically requires that the harmful conduct occurred in the exercise of myndighetsutövning, meaning a one-sided exercise of public power over the individual, a decision or measure binding on them, rather than in some other, non-authoritative capacity the public body might also act in, such as an ordinary commercial activity a municipality happens to also run.",
